  something happens that you [3mweren't[0m watching — someone else edits, or time passes and a deadline arrives.

  So "notified when tasks they care about change" only has real content in one of two worlds:

  - [1mOther people change things[0m → you need multi-user, and the notification is genuinely informative.
  - [1mTime changes things[0m → single-user works fine, but the trigger is a due date or reminder, not a "change" at all.
    That's a [3mreminders[0m feature wearing the word "notifications."

  The second thing to flag: [1mthis is bigger than one project.[0m There are no tasks in this repo. A notification system
  needs a task model, persistence, and a way to express "cares about" — each a subsystem in its own right. I'd decompose
  into (1) tasks + storage, (2) the interest/subscription concept, (3) notification generation and delivery, and spec
  them in that order. Notifications specced against a task model that doesn't exist yet will be fiction.

  My recommendation: [1msingle-user, browser-local, and the real feature is due-date reminders plus an in-page activity log
[0m  — unless other people are genuinely in the picture, in which case multi-user is worth its cost and we should say so
  now rather than retrofit it.

  But I'd rather hear the concrete moment you pictured than pick for you.
